# {{ project_name }}

Install uv, Node 24, pnpm and Go Task.

```sh
task setup
{% if enable_jobs %}
task jobs:redis
# Separate terminal: task jobs:worker
{% endif %}
task dev
```

Open http://127.0.0.1:5173. API health: http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health.

Official shadcn-svelte components are vendored in frontend/src/lib/components/ui. Run `task ui:add -- button dialog` to add registry components. Commit uv/pnpm lockfiles after setup.

## Production and tailnet

Install Caddy, run task build and task serve from the project directory. Caddy binds to loopback on port 8080. For private HTTPS access, authenticate Tailscale, enable MagicDNS and HTTPS, then run task tailscale. Restrict tailnet grants to intended users/devices. Do not use Funnel without introducing application authentication. For direct LAN access explicitly configure a Caddy listener, HTTPS and appropriate access controls.

{% if enable_mcp %}
MCP: streamable HTTP at /mcp/ on the API or Caddy origin; exposes read-only list_notes.
{% endif %}
Native browser notifications require permission and localhost or HTTPS. They work while the page is open; no background push service is included.
{% if enable_terminal %}

## Terminal

Install ttyd (includes xterm.js) on the Linux host. Run as an unprivileged account. Create a strong password hash using Caddy's hash-password command and export it as TERMINAL_PASSWORD_HASH in Caddy's process environment. Run task terminal separately. Caddy protects terminal HTTP and WebSocket routes with basic authentication (username operator). Open through Caddy, not Vite. Do not expose ttyd directly; a shell has the privileges of the host account.
{% endif %}

## Development

task check, task fix, task test, task test:e2e, task build. `task setup` installs Chromium; use `task browser:install` to reinstall or `task browser:install-deps` for Linux OS dependencies if required. Setup installs pre-commit hooks if this project is already a Git repository. Otherwise run task hooks:install after git init.


## Reusable UI

Standalone Svelte 5 + Vite builds a static SPA; FastAPI is the sole application backend. Build output remains frontend/dist. The example notes screen includes TanStack sorting, filtering, pagination, PaneForge resizable/persistent layout, and Zod/TanStack Form validation. The diagnostics screen uses psutil host and process metrics.
{% if enable_events %}

## Live updates

The optional /api/events SSE stream sends current notes on connection and when the database changes, including changes made by another process such as a Taskiq worker. EventSource reconnects automatically. The browser displays a toast and, if granted permission, a native notification. The starter polls SQLite once per second per connected client; it does not provide durable event history or push while the page is closed.
{% endif %}

## Screenshots

Run task screenshots after installing Chromium. The Playwright capture suite saves real PNGs in screenshots/ using a dedicated test database. The source template has a manual Screenshots workflow to publish images.

## API contract and frontend checks

Run task api:generate whenever Python API models or routes change. It exports FastAPI's OpenAPI schema without starting a server, then generates frontend/src/lib/generated/api.d.ts using openapi-typescript. Commit the generated declarations; frontend/openapi.json is a disposable intermediate. Use openapi-fetch via src/lib/api.ts. Never hand-edit generated types. task api:check detects stale declarations; task check runs it along with ESLint, Prettier and svelte-check. task fix applies Ruff, ESLint and Prettier fixes. Setup normalizes the initial frontend formatting; CI validates the generated baseline. Generated declarations and vendored shadcn registry files are excluded from application lint/format rules but are included in type checking and builds.

Settings include persistent accessible switches for success notifications, desktop notifications and reduced motion. Command and context menus include leading icons and retain accessible text labels.
{% if enable_schema_forms %}
The optional schema-generated note form uses SJSF and AJV with the NoteInput schema from generated OpenAPI JSON. The ordinary note form uses TanStack Form; both save to the same API.
{% endif %}

## Server state

TanStack Query owns API response caching, loading/error state, note mutations and cache invalidation. SSE updates the same notes cache when enabled. Do not duplicate API server state in local component state. Diagnostics and health have a short five-second freshness window; other queries default to 30 seconds. Refresh explicitly re-fetches data.

{% if enable_rss %}
## RSS updates

Set APP_PUBLIC_URL in .env to the canonical origin at which subscribers reach the application (for example, the Tailscale HTTPS origin). It defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8080. Publish an explicit announcement using the local CLI:

```sh
task rss:publish -- "Version 1.0" "Initial release" --link https://example.org/releases/v1
```

The RSS 2.0 feed is available at /feed.rss through FastAPI, Vite and Caddy. It includes the latest 50 explicitly published announcements, stable UUID GUIDs and publication dates. Feed discovery metadata and the sidebar/command-palette links are included. The command palette can copy the current browser-origin feed URL. RSS uses ETags and supports conditional requests. Notes, diagnostics, private application records and internal logs are never automatically published. The feed is readable by anyone who can reach its endpoint; it is not an authentication boundary. The CLI is the only publishing interface in this starter. No external feed fetching or reader is included.
{% endif %}

## Task command interface

Run `task --list` to discover commands. Use `task install`, `task setup`, `task dev`, `task build`, `task serve`, `task check`, `task fix`, `task test`, `task test:all`, `task test:e2e`, `task screenshots`, `task benchmark`, `task db:init`, `task api:generate`, `task api:check`. Add packages with `task api:add -- package` or `task ui:dependency:add -- package`. Install platform prerequisites (Go Task, uv, Node, pnpm and any optional system services) first; the Taskfile owns project-level operations, not system package management.
